usb: dwc2: stop freeing the whole periodic schedule for one endpoint

Unplugging the mouse killed the keyboard. The keyboard stayed enumerated and
bound, its interrupt URBs completed -ESHUTDOWN, and nothing was ever scheduled
for it again.

The frame list is the periodic schedule for the WHOLE controller, but
dwc2_hcd_qh_free_ddma() tore it down whenever ANY one periodic endpoint was
freed. The guard was

    (hsotg->params.uframe_sched || !hsotg->periodic_channels)

and this board sets uframe_sched, so the left side is unconditionally true and
the test never guarded anything.

It never came back either. The frame list is only allocated in
dwc2_hcd_qh_init_ddma(), and only for a NEW qh that finds it NULL. Endpoints
that already had a qh simply stopped being scheduled, with
dwc2_update_frame_list() logging "hsotg->frame_list = 00000000" and returning.

That single bug accounts for a fortnight of symptoms that never made sense
together: input dying intermittently, the fault appearing to MOVE between
devices, and both a usbhid rebind and a controller re-probe fixing it
temporarily - each of those creates a new qh, which reallocates the list.

Test it against what is actually left instead. periodic_qh_count is decremented
in dwc2_hcd_qh_unlink() before the free path runs, so here it counts the
endpoints that REMAIN. A caller that ever frees without unlinking now leaks 256
bytes rather than silently stopping all input, which is the safer direction by
a wide margin.

Measured after the fix, keyboard and mouse and hub together: zero
"frame_list = 00000000" complaints in any capture, 310/310 interrupt-IN
completions with status 0, and no bus errors while typing.

Not produced by git diff: linux-71-port/ carries no VCS, so the "before" side
is upstream v7.1 text reconstructed by hand and the hunk was generated with
diff(1) against it.

--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_ddma.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_ddma.c
@@ -373,10 +373,36 @@
 		dwc2_release_channel_ddma(hsotg, qh);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&hsotg->lock, flags);
 
+	/*
+	 * The frame list is the periodic schedule for the WHOLE controller, so
+	 * it may only be torn down once nothing periodic is left. The test used
+	 * to be "uframe_sched || !periodic_channels", and with uframe_sched
+	 * enabled - which it is here - that is unconditionally true. Freeing
+	 * any one periodic endpoint therefore destroyed the schedule for every
+	 * other one.
+	 *
+	 * It never came back. The frame list is only allocated in
+	 * dwc2_hcd_qh_init_ddma(), and only for a NEW qh that finds it NULL;
+	 * endpoints that already had a qh simply stopped being scheduled, with
+	 * dwc2_update_frame_list() logging "hsotg->frame_list = 00000000" and
+	 * returning. The device stayed enumerated and bound, and went quiet.
+	 *
+	 * Observed on this board: unplugging the mouse killed the keyboard.
+	 * The keyboard's interrupt URBs completed -ESHUTDOWN, no further
+	 * transfers were scheduled for it, and rebinding usbhid brought it
+	 * back - because that created a new qh, which reallocated the list.
+	 * That also explains why it looked intermittent and why it appeared to
+	 * move between devices: any teardown of any periodic endpoint takes
+	 * down whichever devices are left.
+	 *
+	 * periodic_qh_count is decremented in dwc2_hcd_qh_unlink() before the
+	 * free path runs, so by here it counts the endpoints that REMAIN. If a
+	 * caller ever frees without unlinking, this leaks 256 bytes rather
+	 * than silently stopping all input - the safer direction by far.
+	 */
 	if ((qh->ep_type == USB_ENDPOINT_XFER_ISOC ||
 	     qh->ep_type == USB_ENDPOINT_XFER_INT) &&
-	    (hsotg->params.uframe_sched ||
-	     !hsotg->periodic_channels) && hsotg->frame_list) {
+	    !hsotg->periodic_qh_count && hsotg->frame_list) {
 		dwc2_per_sched_disable(hsotg);
 		dwc2_frame_list_free(hsotg);
 	}
